466775119304657], [" verify", -1.7854359149932861]]}, {"t": ".", "lp": -0.2588995099067688, "top": [[".", -0.2588995099067688], [" the", -2.082935094833374]]}, {"t": "", "lp": -0.04866568744182587, "top": [["", -0.04866568744182587], ["\n\n", -3.299813747406006]]}], "text": "# CCS Modes on Intel Arc (xe driver)\n\nCCS stands for **Compute and Copy Engine Scheduler**. These modes control how the GPU's compute and copy engines are scheduled and managed, and they're typically set via the `xe.ccs_mode` kernel module parameter.\n\n## The Modes\n\n**CCS Mode 0 (default)**\n- The default mode where the driver manages CCS engines automatically\n- The kernel handles CCS allocation and scheduling without special user intervention\n\n**CCS Mode 1**\n- Enables explicit CCS support, allowing applications to directly request and use dedicated compute/copy engines\n- Useful for workloads that want fine-grained control over engine scheduling, particularly for multi-GPU or high-concurrency scenarios\n\n**CCS Mode 2**\n- Provides another scheduling policy variant, often with different behavior around how CCS engines are distributed or prioritized\n- Typically used for specific performance tuning scenarios\n\n## When to Use Mode 4\n\nI want to be honest here: **I'm not confident about the exact semantics of CCS Mode 4**, and I don't want to give you incorrect information on something this specific. The CCS mode values and their precise meanings have changed across different driver versions, and the documentation isn't always clear or stable.\n\n## My Recommendation\n\nFor accurate, version-specific details, I'd suggest checking:\n\n1. **The kernel source** \u2014 the `xe` driver source in `drivers/gpu/driver/xe/` has the authoritative definitions\n2. **`modinfo xe`** and any module documentation\n3. **Intel's documentation** or the latest driver release notes\n\nWould you like me to help you look up the current values in your specific kernel/driver version instead? If you tell me your kernel version, I can point you toward the right source files to check."}